THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
The Poisson count of single-edge obstructions is not shown to be the consensus event. A uniformly random initial coloring is almost surely not stable and has O(n) crossing edges, so absence of single-edge stable obstructions in the initial configuration does not prevent the dynamics from later reaching a stable mixed configuration with a larger crossing matching. The target's own matching characterization allows stable mixed configurations with many crossing edges, and it gives no argument that these are negligible or that the final matching size is O(1). Thus e^{-λ} is not established as the limiting consensus probability.

